---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/winback";
const title = "Shopify email apps for winback: workflow guide";
const desc = "Identify a meaningful lapse and offer a relevant reason to return.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When is a customer lapsed?",
    "answer": "When their order history and relevant buying cycle support that definition. A universal 90-day delay can misclassify seasonal or durable-goods customers."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should the first message contain a discount?",
    "answer": "Choose from the customer context, offer terms and economics. Neither an immediate discount nor a mandatory proof-first ladder is universally correct."
  },
  {
    "question": "What proves winback worked?",
    "answer": "Comparable return and contribution measures help; a suitable randomized comparison can estimate incrementality. Attributed orders alone do not establish causation."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Winback</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Shopify email apps for winback: workflow guide</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Identify a meaningful lapse and offer a relevant reason to return.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Define purchase lapse separately</h2>
      <p>Purchase winback concerns customers with prior orders. Newsletter re-engagement and product activation are different jobs. Write which qualifying orders and dates establish a lapse, and avoid presenting a predicted churn score as a confirmed cancellation.</p>
      <p>Compare repeat intervals by product and cohort, including customers who have not yet returned. Looking only at repeat buyers hides those still waiting. Use broad, cautious windows where history is thin rather than a fixed category benchmark.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Respect the reason for a long interval</h2>
      <p>Seasonal buyers, gift purchasers and durable-goods owners may have no immediate need. Use explicit gift data where available and label uncertain cases rather than assuming an address mismatch proves a gift.</p>
      <p>A consumable buyer may be following an existing replenishment schedule. A subscription can be active, paused or cancelled for different reasons. Review the relevant product and status before offering a restart or implying the relationship ended.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Set entry, exit and reentry together</h2>
      <p>Record a lapse definition version, last qualifying purchase, eligibility check and series completion state. A new order, withdrawn permission or resolved need should stop obsolete queued messages.</p>
      <p>Define a cooldown and a reason for future reentry, such as a new relevant collection or customer preference change. Do not restart the same sequence forever, but do not assume an annual message or three-touch limit fits every audience.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Choose a credible reason to return</h2>
      <p>Use accurate product improvements, a relevant new collection, practical education or a service invitation. Former VIPs may value recognition, but they are not universally excluded from offers that meet the program’s terms.</p>
      <p>A one-time gift purchaser may prefer another gifting occasion rather than personal-use replenishment. Avoid claiming a product fixes the customer’s prior complaint unless the change is real and the claim is supportable.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Resolve conflicts before scheduling</h2>
      <p>Check recent purchases, active carts, current replenishment reminders, launch campaigns and support issues. Decide whether to defer, combine or omit the actual competing messages, with a clear resume rule.</p>
      <p>A holiday campaign can replace redundant winback content when relevant; it need not disable all winback throughout a calendar month. Coordinate channel frequency and eligibility across senders rather than treating each flow as isolated.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Make offers an economic choice</h2>
      <p>If considering an incentive, calculate expected contribution from net sales after discounts, product costs, fulfillment, returns and messaging costs. Do not subtract the same discount again from already discounted sales.</p>
      <p>Compare access, bundles, service and discounts where suitable. Prior code use does not prove price sensitivity, and non-openers are not automatically the best people to receive deeper offers. An offer should have a stated eligibility rule and a real expiry.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Keep permission and service distinct</h2>
      <p>A prior purchase does not itself establish current email or SMS eligibility. Check channel status at send time and respect the store’s inactive-contact policy rather than importing a dormant file directly into a promotional blast.</p>
      <p>An unresolved complaint may need service recovery instead of a sales message. Preserve essential order communications and earned benefits; do not use a winback suppression flag to hide a support case or erase customer history.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Measure return without claiming every order</h2>
      <p>Report eligible people, delivered messages, purchasers, refunds and contribution over a fixed follow-up window. Count each returning customer once across overlapping branches and separate full-price from discounted orders without treating either as automatically superior.</p>
      <p>Propose a randomized no-winback comparison among suitable eligible customers to estimate incremental return. Size and duration depend on volume and the decision; no fixed holdout percentage guarantees a reliable answer. Explain uncertainty when samples are small.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Choose tools by exit reliability</h2>
      <p>Ask the current sender and alternatives to demonstrate a late purchase cancelling a queued offer, product-specific subscription exclusions, a completed-series cooldown and exportable cohort membership. Confirm current event and plan support in primary documentation.</p>
      <p>Compare maintaining the current workflow with adding a connector or migrating. Include inactive-contact handling, send volume, data work, creative maintenance and any managed service fees; a predictive feature alone does not establish better recovery.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Release a bounded recovery cohort</h2>
      <p>Proposed rehearsal: seasonal purchaser, active subscription on another product, former VIP, new order during a wait, unsubscribed customer and a previously completed series. Write expected decisions before attempting the checks.</p>
      <p>Start with a well-understood eligible cohort and a named pause owner. Carry completion history and suppression state through migration, disable duplicate senders and review both customer response and economics before widening eligibility.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">Audience definitions →</a> · <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">Post-purchase →</a> · <a class="link" href="/use-cases/product-launch">Product launch →</a></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Winback FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
